can be covered by 2 sunny lines, each covering 2 points, with each pair not V/H/D aligned.\n\nThus we need to find a configuration.\n\nLet's try to find a covering for n=5 with 3 non-sunny lines covering 13 points, leaving 2 points that are not V/H/D aligned.\n\nTotal points = 15. So we need to cover 13 points with 3 non-sunny lines. Each non-sunny line can cover at most 5 points (vertical x=1 covers 5). So 3 lines can cover at most 15 points, but due to overlap, we need to be careful.\n\nWe can try to use vertical x=1 (covers 5 points), vertical x=2 (covers 4 points), diagonal sum=6 (covers 5 points). Overlap: vertical x=1 and diagonal sum=6 intersect at (1,5). vertical x=2 and diagonal sum=6 intersect at (2,4). So total covered points = 5+4+5 - 2 (overlap) = 12 points. That's not enough.\n\nTry vertical x=1 (5), diagonal sum=6 (5), diagonal sum=5 (4). Overlap: x=1 with sum=6 at (1,5); x=1 with sum=5 at (1,4). sum=6 and sum=5 intersect at (2,4)? Actually sum=6 and sum=5 intersect at no point (they are parallel lines with different sums). So total covered = 5+5+4 - 2 = 12 points. Not enough.\n\nTry vertical x=1 (5), vertical x=3 (3), diagonal sum=6 (5). Overlap: x=1 with sum=6 at (1,5); x=3 with sum=6 at (3,3). So total = 5+3+5 - 2 = 11 points.\n\nTry vertical x=1 (5), vertical x=2 (4), vertical x=3 (3). Overlap none (different columns). Total = 5+4+3 = 12 points.\n\nTry vertical x=1 (5), diagonal sum=6 (5), diagonal sum=4 (3). Overlap: x=1 with sum=6 at (1,5); x=1 with sum=4 at (1,3). So total = 5+5+3 - 2 = 11.\n\nThus using vertical lines and diagonals seems to give at most 12 points covered.\n\nBut maybe we can use horizontal lines. Horizontal y=1 covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1) (5 points). Horizontal y=2 covers (1,2),(2,2),(3,2),(4,2) (4 points). Horizontal y=3 covers (1,3),(2,3),(3,3) (3 points). So using three horizontals y=1,y=2,y=3 covers 5+4+3 = 12 points (no overlap). That's 12 points. So we need to cover 3 more points with sunny lines. But we only have 2 sunny lines. So fails.\n\nWhat about using a mix: vertical x=1 (5), horizontal y=1 (5), diagonal sum=6 (5). Overlap: x=1 and y=1 intersect at (1,1); x=1 and sum=6 intersect at (1,5); y=1 and sum=6 intersect at (5,1). So total covered = 5+5+5 - 3 = 12 points. So still 3 points left.\n\nThus seems difficult to cover 13 points with 3 non-sunny lines. But maybe we can use a diagonal sum=5 (covers 4 points) plus vertical x=1 (5) plus vertical x=2 (4). Overlap: x=1 with sum=5 at (1,4); x=2 with sum=5 at (2,3). So total = 5+4+4 - 2 = 11 points.\n\nThus maximum coverage with 3 non-sunny lines seems to be 12 points for n=5. Let's verify if any combination can cover 13 points. The maximum coverage per line is 5 (vertical x=1 or horizontal y=1). So three lines can cover at most 15 points if they are disjoint. But they cannot be disjoint because the region is triangular, and any two lines from V/H/D families intersect at at most one point (if they are not parallel). So we can try to choose three lines that intersect minimally. For example, choose vertical x=1 (covers 5 points), horizontal y=1 (covers 5 points), diagonal sum=6 (covers 5 points). Overlaps: each pair intersect at one point: (1,1) for x=1 & y=1, (1,5) for x=1 & sum=6, (5,1) for y=1 & sum=6. So total covered = 5+5+5 - 3 = 12. That's the maximum we can get with three lines each covering 5 points, because any two lines intersect at exactly one point (since they are not parallel). So the total covered is 5+5+5 - 3 = 12. So we cannot cover more than 12 points with 3 non-sunny lines for n=5. Indeed, the maximum coverage is 12.\n\nThus we will always have at least 3 points uncovered after using 3 non-sunny lines. Since we have 2 sunny lines left, we need to cover at least 3 points with 2 sunny lines. That's possible if we can pair two of the points on a sunny line, and the third point on the other sunny line. So we need to find a configuration where the leftover 3 points contain a pair that is not V/H/D aligned. Let'