, ..., column n has height 1.\n\nIf we have n-k non-sunny lines, we can allocate them to cover columns. Suppose we use vertical lines for some columns, covering those columns fully. For the remaining columns, we need to cover each point using non-vertical lines (horizontal, diagonal, sunny). Each non-vertical line can cover at most one point per column. So for a column of height h, we need at least h lines intersecting that column (unless we have a vertical line). So the total number of non-vertical lines needed to cover all points in columns without vertical lines is sum_{a in S} (n+1-a), where S is the set of columns without vertical lines. But we have n-k non-sunny lines total, which includes vertical lines (if any) and non-vertical lines (horizontal, diagonal, sunny). However, vertical lines are also non-sunny lines. So we need to allocate n-k lines among vertical and non-vertical lines.\n\nBut we can think of the following: Let V be the set of columns with a vertical line. Then |V| ≤ n-k (since each vertical line uses one non-sunny line). The remaining n-k - |V| non-sunny lines are non-vertical lines (horizontal, diagonal, sunny). For each column a not in V, we need to cover its points using non-vertical lines. Each non-vertical line can cover at most one point per column. So the total number of points in columns not in V must be ≤ (n-k - |V|) * (max number of columns each non-vertical line can intersect). Actually each non-vertical line can intersect each column at most once, but can intersect many columns. So a single non-vertical line can cover one point in each column (if it passes through a point in that column). So a non-vertical line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover points across many columns. So the total number of points in columns not in V must be ≤ (n-k - |V|) * (number of columns not in V). Actually each non-vertical line can cover at most one point in each column, so across all columns not in V, each non-vertical line can cover